alization. They may be set by us or by third parties whose services we use on our sites. If you do not allow these services, this website may not function properly. Back Button Cookie List Search Icon Filter Icon Clear checkbox label label Apply Cancel Consent Leg.Interest checkbox label label checkbox label label checkbox label label Reject All Confirm My Choices 1 Technical Support Today New Messages Technical Support Chatbot (with creds) a few seconds ago Welcome to Technical Support. Please could you let us know whether you're a candidate or a recruiter? Candidate Recruiter \u26a1 by Freshworks 1"}, "https://jobs.lse.ac.uk/Vacancies/W/4608/0/474943/15539/research-assistant-projecting-future-ethnic-diversity-and-social-attitudes-in-britain": {"title": "Research Assistant, Projecting Future Ethnic Diversity and Social Attitudes in Britain", "description": "Research Assistant, Projecting Future Ethnic Diversity and Social Attitudes in Britain Register Login Back to search results Research Assistant, Projecting Future Ethnic Diversity and Social Attitudes in Britain LSE is committed to building a diverse, equitable and truly inclusive university The Centre for Economic Performance Research Assistant, Projecting Future Ethnic Diversity and Social Attitudes in Britain Salary from \u00a336,513 to \u00a341,565 pa with potential to progress to \u00a344,468 pa inclusive of London allowance Fixed term appointment for 1 year (with possibility of extension for up to 2 years) You will be required to work on the Nuffield funded project \u201cProjecting Future Ethnic Diversity and Social Attitudes in Britain\u201d in the Centre for Economic Performance, and associated core Centre projects. The project will develop models for projecting future ethnic diversity in Britain and exploring the likely implications of rising diversity for social attitudes on a wide range of issues. A n undergraduate degree in Economics, Statistics or other quantitative discipline to be completed by post start date is essential, as is experience of data work requiring great accuracy and attention to detail. Essential criteria include: advanced experience of using programming languages such as STATA and R for analysing large scale micro data sets and the ability to learn new programming skills and use AI in an accurate, productive way; experience of data work requiring great accuracy and attention to detail; ability to identify appropriate methods of statistical investigation; ability to interpret and analyse patterns or trends in data; and ability to think through problems analytically; ability to perform literature reviews. The ability to work independently without close supervision is a requirement, as well as excellent written and verbal communication skills. The post will commence as soon as possible. You should apply with a CV, cover letter as well one relevant or sample research paper The CEP offers an outstanding environment in which to carry out research. Please visit http://cep.lse.ac.uk/ for further information about the Centre. We offer an occupational pension scheme, generous annual leave and excellent training and development opportunities. For further information about the post, please see the how to apply document , information for applicants document , job description and the person specification . If you have any technical queries with applying on the online system, please use the \u201ccontact us\u201d links at the bottom of the LSE Jobs page. Should you have any queries about the role, please email c.h.tocock@lse.ac.uk The closing date for receipt of applications is 18 October 2026 (23.59 UK time). Regrettably, we are unable to accept any late applications. Apply now Back to search results Research Assistant, Projecting Future Ethnic Diversity and Social Attitudes in Britain Job Type: Research Posted: 25/09/2026 Closing date: 18/10/2026 Closing time: 23:59 (BST) Apply now Save Share Login Register Email Address: Please enter a valid email address. Password: Sign in Forgot password? Your password has expired, please create a new password below. Your email address has not been verified. Please check your email or click here to resend. Verification email has been resent. Email Address: Please enter a valid email address. Reset password Cancel Email Address: New Password: At least 8 characters Uppercase Lowercase Symbols Numbers Confirm Password: Update Password Your login attempt was not successful. Please try again Please select Baroness Baron Doctor Father Lady Lord Miss Mr Mrs Ms Professor Dame Professor Professor Lord Professor Sir Reverend Sir Other Dame Mx Title * First name * Last name * Email address * Password * At least 8 characters Uppercase Lowercase Symbols Numbers Confirm password * I have read and understood the terms and conditions I have read and understood the data protection statement Create Account Cancel An email has been sent to your email address. Please confirm that your email address is active by clicking the link in the email. Powered by engage|ats Havas People a Havas Company This site is protected by reCAPTCHA. Contact Us - Application Query Terms and Conditions Privacy Policy Cookies Accessibility FAQs"}, "https://jobs.lse.ac.uk/Vacancies/W/2016/0/475166/15539/research-fellow-econometrics-and-validation": {"title": "Research Fellow (Econometrics and Validation)", "description": "Research Fellow (Econometrics and Validation) Register Login Back to search results Research Fellow (Econometrics and Validation) LSE is committed to building a diverse, equitable and truly inclusive university The Inclusion Initiative (TII) Department of Psychological and Behavioural Science Research Fellow (Econometrics and Validation) Salary from \u00a353,949 to \u00a362,160 pa inclusive with potential to progress to \u00a369,073 pa inclusive of London allowance Funding is available to support this position for 1 year, starting Autumn 2026 The Inclusion Initiative (TII) at LSE uses behavioural science and rigorous quantitative research to help organisations build more inclusive workplaces. Its Growth and Governance (G&G) Hub is dedicated to measuring and driving progress on race inclusion in large listed companies. The Hub has assembled every annual report published by FTSE 350 companies over a seven-year period and built the first large, expert-calibrated measure of how those companies write about diversity. Around 20 diversity and inclusion specialists hand-labelled more than 25,000 passages to create a ground truth; a large language model was then tested against that ground truth and tuned until it matched the experts as closely as the experts matched one another. That model now scores the full corpus, producing a comparable, independently produced measure for every report. The first company reports to be published concern race and ethnicity. Further information about The Inclusion Initiative is available at www.lse.ac.uk/tii . Applications are invited for a Research Fellow to lead the quantitative validation of the Hub's measures and to write that work up for publication. You will report to Dr Grace Lordan, Director of TII. You will assemble and link firm-level data from external sources, including financial performance, workforce composition and diversity, and governance characteristics, and establish the relationship between the Hub's measures and those variables using panel methods appropriate to a multi-year corpus. You will test the construct validity, robustness and stability of the measure and its components, propose improvements, and contribute to peer reviewed papers and published methodology that open the measure to external scrutiny. Candidates should have: \u00b7 a PhD in economics, econometrics, quantitative social science or a closely related subject, or expect to have submitted their PhD thesis by the post start date; \u00b7 a solid background in applied econometrics, in particular panel data methods; \u00b7 A track record or trajectory of peer-reviewed publications; \u00b7 demonstrable expertise in research on diversity and inclusion, including a strong grounding in race and ethnicity in workplace, labour market or organisational settings; \u00b7 experience of constructing and linking large firm-level datasets from multiple sources, including firm-level, financial or administrative data, and advanced programming skills in Stata, R and/or Python. We offer an occupational pension scheme, generous annual leave, hybrid working, and excellent training and development opportunities. The successful candidate is expected to be in the office in London a minimum of two days per week as chosen by the Director. For further information about the post, please see the how to apply document , job description and the person specification . To apply for this post, please go to www.jobs.lse.ac.uk . If you have any technical queries with applying on the online system, please use the \u201ccontact us\u201d links at the bottom of the LSE Jobs page. Should you have any queries about the role, please email TII@lse.ac.uk The closing date for receipt of applications is 18 October 2026 (23:59 UK time). Regrettably, we are unable to accept any late applications. Apply now Back to search results Research Fellow (Econometrics and Validation) Job Type: Research Posted: 02/10/2026 Closing date: 18/10/2026 Closing time: 23:59 (BST) Apply now Save Share Login Register Email Address: Please enter a valid email address. Password: Sign in Forgot password? Your password has expired, please create a new password below. Your email address has not been verified. Please check your email or click here to resend. Verification email has been resent. Email Address: Please enter a valid email address. Reset password Cancel Email Address: New Password: At least 8 characters Uppercase Lowercase Symbols Numbers Confirm Password: Update Password Your login attempt was not successful. Please try again Please select Baroness Baron Doctor Father Lady Lord Miss Mr Mrs Ms Professor Dame Professor Professor Lord Professor Sir Reverend Sir Other Dame Mx Title * First name * Last name * Email address * Password * At least 8 characters Uppercase Lowercase Symbols Numbers Confirm password * I have read and understood the terms and conditions I have read and understood the data protection statement Create Account Cancel An email has been sent to your email address. Please confirm that your email address is active by clicking the link in the email. Powered by engage|ats Havas People a Havas Company This site is protected by reCAPTCHA. Contact Us - Application Query Terms and Conditions Privacy Policy Cookies Accessibility FAQs"}, "https://jobs.lse.ac.uk/Vacancies/W/1604/0/475156/15539/research-fellow-reporting-and-impact": {"title": "Research Fellow (Reporting and Impact)", "description": "Research Fellow (Reporting and Impact) Register Login Back to search results Research Fellow (Reporting and Impact) LSE is committed to building a diverse, equitable and truly inclusive university The Inclusion Initiative (TII) Department of Psychological and Behavioural Science Research Fellow (Reporting and Impact) Salary from \u00a353,949 to \u00a362,160 pa inclusive with potential to progress to \u00a369,073 pa inclusive of London allowance Funding is available to support this position for 1 year, starting Autumn 2026 The Inclusion Initiative (TII) at LSE uses behavioural science and rigorous quantitative research to help organisations build more inclusive workplaces. Its Growth and Governance (G&G) Hub is dedicated to measuring and driving progress on race inclusion in large listed companies. The Hub assembled every annual report published by FTSE 350 companies over a seven-year period and built the first large, expert-calibrated measure of how those companies write about diversity. Diversity and inclusion specialists hand-labelled more than 25,000 passages to create a ground truth; a LLM was then tested against that ground truth and tuned until it matched the experts as closely as the experts matched one another. That model now scores the full corpus, producing a comparable, independently-produced measure for every reports, the first published of which concern race and ethnicity. Further information about The Inclusion Initiative is available at www.lse.ac.uk/tii . Applications are invited for a Research Fellow to act as editor of the Hub's programme of company reports and to lead its stakeholder engagement and impact activity. You will work under the direction of Dr Grace Lordan. You will work alongside the colleagues who generate the underlying data, quality assuring their outputs and producing, editing and publishing up to 700 semi-automated company reports. You will lead outreach to these companies and to investors, regulators, membership bodies and practitioner networks, and you will be accountable for the Hub's engagement and impact targets. Candidates should have: \u00b7 Completion of a PhD in a relevant social science discipline, for example behavioural science, psychology, economics, management or sociology (or expect to have submitted their PhD thesis by the post start date) or substantive industry experience; \u00b7 demonstrable expertise in research on diversity and inclusion, including a strong grounding in race and ethnicity in workplace, labour market or organisational settings; \u00b7 a record of producing high-quality written outputs for practitioner/policy audiences; \u00b7 experience of working with large datasets, together with a working understanding of how large language models are used to classify or annotate text, sufficient to interpret their outputs, identify errors and describe the method accurately to non-technical audiences; \u00b7 excellent written and oral communication skills; and the credibility to present findings to senior external audiences. We offer an occupational pension scheme, generous annual leave, hybrid working, and excellent training and development opportunities. The successful candidate is expected to be in the office in London a minimum of two days per week as chosen by the Director. For further information about the post, please see the how to apply document , job description and the person specification . To apply for this post, please go to www.jobs.lse.ac.uk . If you have any technical queries with applying on the online system, please use the \u201ccontact us\u201d links at the bottom of the LSE Jobs page. Should you have any queries about the role, please email TII@lse.ac.uk The closing date for receipt of applications is 18 October 2026 (23:59 UK time). Regrettably, we are unable to accept any late applications. Apply now Back to search results Research Fellow (Reporting and Impact) Job Type: Research Posted: 02/10/2026 Closing date: 18/10/2026 Closing time: 23:59 (BST) Apply now Save Share Login Register Email Address: Please enter a valid email address. Password: Sign in Forgot password? Your password has expired, please create a new password below. Your email address has not been verified. Please check your email or click here to resend. Verification email has been resent. Email Address: Please enter a valid email address. Reset password Cancel Email Address: New Password: At least 8 characters Uppercase Lowercase Symbols Numbers Confirm Password: Update Password Your login attempt was not successful. Please try again Please select Baroness Baron Doctor Father Lady Lord Miss Mr Mrs Ms Professor Dame Professor Professor Lord Professor Sir Reverend Sir Other Dame Mx Title * First name * Last name * Email address * Password * At least 8 characters Uppercase Lowercase Symbols Numbers Confirm password * I have read and understood the terms and conditions I have read and understood the data protection statement Create Account Cancel An email has been sent to your email address. Please confirm that your email address is active by clicking the link in the email. Powered by engage|ats Havas People a Havas Company This site is protected by reCAPTCHA. Contact Us - Application Query Terms and Conditions Privacy Policy Cookies Accessibility FAQs"}, "https://jobs.lse.ac.uk/Vacancies/W/5540/0/475176/15539/platforms-engineering-lead": {"title": "Platforms Engineering Lead", "description": "Platforms Engineering Lead Register Login Back to search results Platforms Engineering Lead LSE is committed to building a diverse, equitable and truly inclusive university Data and Technology Services Platforms Engineering Lead Salary from \u00a353,949 to \u00a362,160 pa inclusive with potential to progress to \u00a369,073 pa inclusive of London allowance The London School of Economics is a cosmopolitan university situated in the vibrant centre of London that specialises in the study of social sciences. LSE\u2019s Data and Technology Services division provides services to over 10,000 staff and student users. The Platforms Engineering Lead provides hands-on technical leadership and day-to-day operational oversight for the School\u2019s platforms and infrastructure services, including compute, storage, virtualisation, backup and cloud foundations. The role is responsible for ensuring these services are reliable, secure, scalable and well-run, while progressively modernising how the platform estate is built and operated. The postholder is responsible for embedding automation, infrastructure-as-code, observability, platform reliability and continuous improvement, while maintaining the stability, scalability and recoverability of a large, business-critical platform estate. The role drives the adoption of reusable platform services, self-service capabilities and standardised engineering approaches that enable DTS and business-led technology teams to deliver and operate solutions more efficiently, while maintaining appropriate security, governance and operational controls. The role will also lead the rest of the team by example and set high service, interaction and delivery standards. Candidates should have: Significant experience (minimum 5+ years) in platform engineering, infrastructure operations or cloud services, including responsibility for business-critical platform services, with evidence of technical leadership capability, mentoring, team coordination or delivery of complex initiatives. Demonstrable technical leadership across platform domains including compute, virtualisation, storage, backup, identity services, cloud platforms and platform automation, with the ability to set technical direction and support the development of others. Strong practical experience implementing automation, infrastructure-as-code and platform engineering approaches to improve consistency, reduce manual effort and enable scalable service delivery. We offer an occupational pension scheme, generous annual leave, hybrid working, and excellent training and development opportunities. For further information about the post, please see the how to apply document , job description and the person specification . To apply for this post, please go to www.jobs.lse.ac.uk . If you have any technical queries with applying on the online system, please use the \u201ccontact us\u201d links at the bottom of the LSE Jobs page. Should you have any queries about the role, please email dts.admin@lse.ac.uk . The closing date for receipt of applications is 16 October 2026 (23.59 UK time). Regrettably, we are unable to accept any late applications. Apply now Back to search results Platforms Engineering Lead Job Type: Professional Services Posted: 01/10/2026 Closing date: 16/10/2026 Closing time: 23:59 (BST) Apply now Save Share Login Register Email Address: Please enter a valid email address. Password: Sign in Forgot password? Your password has expired, please create a new password below. Your email address has not been verified. Please check your email or click here to resend. Verification email has been resent. Email Address: Please enter a valid email address. Reset password Cancel Email Address: New Password: At least 8 characters Uppercase Lowercase Symbols Numbers Confirm Password: Update Password Your login attempt was not successful. Please try again Please select Baroness Baron Doctor Father Lady Lord Miss Mr Mrs Ms Professor Dame Professor Professor Lord Professor Sir Reverend Sir Other Dame Mx Title * First name * Last name * Email address * Password * At least 8 characters Uppercase Lowercase Symbols Numbers Confirm password * I have read and understood the terms and conditions I have read and understood the data protection statement Create Account Cancel An email has been sent to your email address. Please confirm that your email address is active by clicking the link in the email. Powered by engage|ats Havas People a Havas Company This site is protected by reCAPTCHA. Contact Us - Application Query Terms and Conditions Privacy Policy Cookies Accessibility FAQs By clicking \u201cAccept All Cookies\u201d, you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to enhance site navigation, analyze site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. View our privacy policy Cookies Settings Reject All Accept All Cookies Privacy Preference Center When you visit any website, it may store or retrieve information on your browser, mostly in the form of cookies. This information might be about you, your preferences or your device and is mostly used to make the site work as you expect it to. The information does not usually directly identify you, but it can give you a more personalized web experience. Because we respect your right to privacy, you can choose not to allow some types of cookies. Click on the different category headings to find out more and change our default settings. However, blocking some types of cookies may impact your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer. Allow All Manage Consent Preferences Strictly Necessary Services Always Active These Services are necessary for the website to function and cannot be switched off in our systems. They are usually only set in response to actions made by you which amount to a request for services, such as setting your privacy preferences, logging in or filling in forms. You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work. Functional Services Functional Services With these services, the website is able to provide advanced functionality and personalization. They may be set by us or by third parties whose services we use on our sites. If you do not allow these services, this website may not function properly. Back Button Cookie List Search Icon Filter Icon Clear checkbox label label Apply Cancel Consent Leg.Interest checkbox label label checkbox label label checkbox label label Reject All Confirm My Choices 1 Technical Support Today New Messages Technical Support Chatbot (with creds) a few seconds ago Welcome to Technical Support. Please could you let us know whether you're a candidate or a recruiter? Candidate Recruiter \u26a1 by Freshworks 1"}, "https://jobs.lse.ac.uk/Vacancies/W/4926/0/475867/15539/assistant-professor-in-management": {"title": "Assistant Professor in Management", "description": "Assistant Professor in Management Register Login Back to search results Assistant Professor in Management LSE is committed to building a diverse, equitable and truly inclusive university As an equal opportunities employer strongly committed to diversity and inclusion, we\u00a0encourage applications from women and people from ethnic minority backgrounds as they are currently under-represented at this level in this area. All appointments will be made on merit or skill and experience relative to the role. Department of Management Assistant Professor in Management Salary is competitive with Departments at our peer institutions worldwide and not less than \u00a368,087 pa inclusive, and the salary scale can be found on the LSE website In addition, this post will attract a significant market salary supplement which reflects current market conditions. The Department of Management holds a central position within LSE, a globally recognized university specializing in the social sciences, located in the heart of London. The department's faculty and research expertise are focused on various areas, including marketing, information systems and innovation, employment relations and human resource management, organisational behaviour, and managerial economics and strategy. Faculty members actively engage in research and scholarly activities across LSE, collaborating with research centres such as the Centre for Economic Performance, the Behavioural Lab for Research and Teaching, and various interdisciplinary institutes. The Department\u2019s degree portfolio includes the BSc Management, a Master\u2019s in Management, and a number of specialist Master\u2019s programmes, including the MSc in Marketing. Successful applicants will have or be close to obtaining a PhD, in Marketing or a closely related discipline, such as Psychology, Economics, Statistics, or Computer Science, by the post start date. We welcome applications from scholars with clearly defined quantitative or behavioural interests. In addition, candidates should demonstrate a clear, well developed, and viable strategy for future outstanding research that has the potential to result in world-leading publications in Marketing. Candidates should also be able to demonstrate excellent written and oral communication skills and have the ability to teach on undergraduate and postgraduate programmes in Management. The other criteria that will be used when shortlisting for this post can be found on the person specification, which is attached to this vacancy on the LSE\u2019s online recruitment system. In addition to a competitive salary the benefits that come with this job include an occupational pension scheme, a research incentive scheme with personal reward options, generous research leave (sabbatical) entitlement, a collegial faculty environment and excellent support, training and development opportunities. For further information about the post, please see the how to apply document , job description and the person specification . To apply for this post, please go to www.jobs.lse.ac.uk . If you have any technical queries with applying on the online system, please use the \u201ccontact us\u201d links at the bottom of the LSE Jobs page. Should you have any queries about the role, please email dom.facultyaffairs@lse.ac.uk The closing date for receipt of applications is 16 November 2026 (23.59 UK time). W e are unable to accept any late applications. Apply now Back to search results Assistant Professor in Management Job Type: Academic Posted: 15/10/2026 Closing date: 16/11/2026 Closing time: 23:59 (GMT) Apply now Save Share Login Register Email Address: Please enter a valid email address. Password: Sign in Forgot password? Your password has expired, please create a new password below. Your email address has not been verified. Please check your email or click here to resend. Verification email has been resent. Email Address: Please enter a valid email address. Reset password Cancel Email Address: New Password: At least 8 characters Uppercase Lowercase Symbols Numbers Confirm Password: Update Password Your login attempt was not successful. Please try again Please select Baroness Baron Doctor Father Lady Lord Miss Mr Mrs Ms Professor Dame Professor Professor Lord Professor Sir Reverend Sir Other Dame Mx Title * First name * Last name * Email address * Password * At least 8 characters Uppercase Lowercase Symbols Numbers Confirm password * I have read and understood the terms and conditions I have read and understood the data protection statement Create Account Cancel An email has been sent to your email address. Please confirm that your email address is active by clicking the link in the email. Powered by engage|ats Havas People a Havas Company This site is protected by reCAPTCHA. Contact Us - Application Query Terms and Conditions Privacy Policy Cookies Accessibility FAQs"}, "https://jobs.lse.ac.uk/Vacancies/W/5138/0/475866/15539/research-officers-for-the-global-priorities-research-group-gprg": {"title": "Research Officers for the Global Priorities Research Group (GPRG)", "description": "Research Officers for the Global Priorities Research Group (GPRG) Register Login Back to search results Research Officers for the Global Priorities Research Group (GPRG) LSE is committed to building a diverse, equitable and truly inclusive university Centre for Philosophy of Natural and Social Science 2 x Research Officers Salary from \u00a343,277 pa to \u00a349,573 pa inclusive with potential to progress to \u00a351,714 pa inclusive of London allowance. Funding is available to support this position for a total 4 years for each position Two postdoctoral researchers are being recruited for the Global Priorities Research Group (GPRG) led by Associate Professorial Research Fellow Andreas L. Mogensen. \u00a0Each postdoctoral researcher will be hosted at the Centre for Philosophy of Natural and Social Science (CPNSS) at the LSE. Each position is tenable in the first instance for four years. GPRG is a newly established research group at CPNSS, and successful candidates will have the opportunity to help shape its research agenda and strategic direction. The successful candidate will conduct innovative and significant research as part of the project team, working collaboratively with Andreas L. Mogensen and other members of the research group, as well as other collaborators at the LSE or elsewhere. At least 50% of their time will be spent on topics directly relevant to the group\u2019s research agenda, which they will help to set. The research group\u2019s mission is to pursue philosophical research relevant to identifying, prioritising among, and addressing the world\u2019s most pressing problems, with particular emphasis on examining the priorities suggested by impartial considerations of wellbeing and ethical questions arising therefrom. The ability to produce research related to large-scale risks and opportunities associated with the advent of transformative artificial intelligence is desirable but not essential. The successful applicant should have: successfully completed or nearly completed a PhD in philosophy (or another discipline relevant to the project themes). a record or trajectory of excellent publications and proven ability or potential to publish in top journals or with leading book publishers in philosophy. a strong research interest in topics that fall within the project remit. the ability to work in a research team, collaborating harmoniously with other team members. Ideally, the successful applicant will begin in September 2027 . For more information about the Global Priorities Research Group, please see: https://www.lse.ac.uk/global-priorities We offer an occupational pension scheme, generous annual leave, hybrid working, and excellent training and development opportunities. For further information about the post, please see the how to apply document, job description and the person specification . To apply for this post, please go to www.jobs.lse.ac.uk . Applications should include a CV, a Cover Letter of no more than 550 words, a writing sample of no more than 10,000 words (including footnotes, references, and bibliography) representing their best and most polished work, and a Research Statement of no more than 550 words describing one or more research topics they would ideally like to pursue as part of the group, and the connection of those topics to their past and current research. If you have any technical queries with applying on the online system, please use the \u201ccontact us\u201d links at the bottom of the LSE Jobs page. Should you have any queries about the role, please email Andreas L. Mogensen at a.mogensen@lse.ac.uk The closing date for receipt of applications is the 15 th of November 2026 (23.59 UK time). Regrettably, we are unable to accept any late applications. Apply now Back to search results Research Officers for the Global Priorities Research Group (GPRG) Job Type: Research Posted: 15/10/2026 Closing date: 15/11/2026 Closing time: 23:59 (GMT) Apply now Save Share Login Register Email Address: Please enter a valid email address. Password: Sign in Forgot password? Your password has expired, please create a new password below. Your email address has not been verified. Please check your email or click here to resend. Verification email has been resent. Email Address: Please enter a valid email address. Reset password Cancel Email Address: New Password: At least 8 characters Uppercase Lowercase Symbols Numbers Confirm Password: Update Password Your login attempt was not successful. Please try again Please select Baroness Baron Doctor Father Lady Lord Miss Mr Mrs Ms Professor Dame Professor Professor Lord Professor Sir Reverend Sir Other Dame Mx Title * First name * Last name * Email address * Password * At least 8 characters Uppercase Lowercase Symbols Numbers Confirm password * I have read and understood the terms and conditions I have read and understood the data protection statement Create Account Cancel An email has been sent to your email address. Please confirm that your email address is active by clicking the link in the email. Powered by engage|ats Havas People a Havas Company This site is protected by reCAPTCHA. Contact Us - Application Query Terms and Conditions Privacy Policy Cookies Accessibility FAQs By clicking \u201cAccept All Cookies\u201d, you agree to the storing of cookies on your device to enhance site navigation, analyze site usage, and assist in our marketing efforts. View our privacy policy Cookies Settings Reject All Accept All Cookies Privacy Preference Center When you visit any website, it may store or retrieve information on your browser, mostly in the form of cookies. This information might be about you, your preferences or your device and is mostly used to make the site work as you expect it to. The information does not usually directly identify you, but it can give you a more personalized web experience. Because we respect your right to privacy, you can choose not to allow some types of cookies. Click on the different category headings to find out more and change our default settings. However, blocking some types of cookies may impact your experience of the site and the services we are able to offer. Allow All Manage Consent Preferences Strictly Necessary Services Always Active These Services are necessary for the website to function and cannot be switched off in our systems. They are usually only set in response to actions made by you which amount to a request for services, such as setting your privacy preferences, logging in or filling in forms. You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work. Functional Services Functional Services With these services, the website is able to provide advanced functionality and personalization. They may be set by us or by third parties whose services we use on our sites. If you do not allow these services, this website may not function properly. Back Button Cookie List Search Icon Filter Icon Clear checkbox label label Apply Cancel Consent Leg.Interest checkbox label label checkbox label label checkbox label label Reject All Confirm My Choices 1 Technical Support Today New Messages Technical Support Chatbot (with creds) a few seconds ago Welcome to Technical Support. Please could you let us know whether you're a candidate or a recruiter? Candidate Recruiter \u26a1 by Freshworks 1"}, "https://jobs.lse.ac.uk/Vacancies/W/1710/0/475804/15539/digital-manager": {"title": "Digital Manager", "description": "Digital Manager Register Login Back to search results Digital Manager LSE is committed to building a diverse, equitable and truly inclusive university Communications Division Digital Manager Salary from \u00a343,277 to \u00a351,714 pa inclusive, with potential to progress to \u00a355,497 pa inclusive of London allowance Permanent, full-time post (5 days per week) This is an opportunity to play a key role in the delivery, support and continuous improvement of LSE's digital services, working as part of the Digital Communications team within the Communications Division. The team manages platforms supporting the main LSE website, blogs and a student-facing application, alongside analytics, search tools and cookie consent and compliance systems. This is a hands-on Digital Manager role with a strong focus on digital service operations and Helpdesk support. You will work with staff, students and the LSE editor community to manage support enquiries, troubleshoot issues and improve the day-to-day operation of LSE's digital platforms. You will also help improve the Helpdesk through better workflows, guidance, documentation and response templates. Alongside this operational focus, you will take ownership of digital projects and workstreams, including cookie consent and compliance, working with internal teams and external suppliers to improve services. Candidates should have: Strong experience supporting digital platforms and CMS services , ideally within a complex organisation, with excellent knowledge of enterprise CMS platforms such as Contensis, WordPress or equivalent. Experience managing digital support enquiries , including triage, troubleshooting, resolution tracking and communicating effectively with staff, students and content editors. A proactive approach to digital service improvement , with the ability to identify recurring issues, improve workflows and processes, and develop practical guidance and ways of working. Experience supporting digital projects and workstreams , including planning and delivery, and working with internal teams and external suppliers to implement digital and technical updates. Experience using analytics and insight tools , including Google Analytics and Google Tag Manager, to monitor performance, produce reports and inform improvements. We offer an occupational pension scheme, generous annual leave and excellent training and development opportunities. For further information about the post, please see the how to apply document , job description and the person specification . To apply for this post, please go to https://jobs.lse.ac.uk . If you have any technical queries with applying on the online system, please use the \u201ccontact us\u201d links at the bottom of the LSE Jobs page. Should you have any queries about the role, please email Jamie Seenan at j.seenan@lse.ac.uk. The closing date for receipt of applications is 10 November 2026 (23.59 UK time). Regrettably, we are unable to accept any late applications. This position will be subject to a standard Disclosure and Barring Service (DBS) check. Any offer of employment made is conditional on receipt of a satisfactory DBS check. Apply now Back to search results Digital Manager Job Type: Professional Services Posted: 15/10/2026 Closing date: 10/11/2026 Closing time: 23:59 (GMT) Apply now Save Share Login Register Email Address: Please enter a valid email address. Password: Sign in Forgot password? Your password has expired, please create a new password below. Your email address has not been verified. Please check your email or click here to resend. Verification email has been resent. Email Address: Please enter a valid email address. Reset password Cancel Email Address: New Password: At least 8 characters Uppercase Lowercase Symbols Numbers Confirm Password: Update Password Your login attempt was not successful. Please try again Please select Baroness Baron Doctor Father Lady Lord Miss Mr Mrs Ms Professor Dame Professor Professor Lord Professor Sir Reverend Sir Other Dame Mx Title * First name * Last name * Email address * Password * At least 8 characters Uppercase Lowercase Symbols Numbers Confirm password * I have read and understood the terms an